<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maoism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1935 CE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maoism, officially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> Thought, is Mao Zedong’s adaptation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a> to agrarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, built on peasant revolution, people’s war and the mass line.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maoism.jpg" alt="Maoism" width="330" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Public_Proclamations_of_July_3_and_July_24_Are_Chairman_Mao's_Great_Strategic_Plans!,_People's_Republic_of_China,_1968,_lithograph_-_Jordan_Schnitzer_Museum_of_Art_-_Eugene,_Oregon_-_DSC09536.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Maoism, officially Mao Zedong Thought, developed while Mao Zedong tried to realise a socialist revolution in the agricultural, pre-industrial society of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. The English term was coined in 1951 by Benjamin I. Schwartz, who used it for Mao's pragmatic strategy of revolution, with a disciplined and hierarchical Communist Party supported by a mobilised peasant mass base.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>, Mao and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> were headquartered in the Yan'an Soviet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. In these years Mao established himself as a Marxist theoretician and wrote most of the works later canonised as the Thought of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>. The period also established his and the party's ideological independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Maoism differs from conventional European Marxism in looking to the agrarian countryside rather than to urban industrial workers. Its theory of New Democracy held that the long road to socialism could be opened only by a national, popular, democratic, anti-feudal and anti-imperialist revolution run by the communists. Its idea of people's war means mobilising large rural populations for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare and surrounding the cities from the countryside. The mass line is summarised as from the masses, to the masses. In 1974 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> announced its Three Worlds Theory at the UN.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>From the 1950s until the reform and opening up of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> in the late 1970s, Maoism was the political and military ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> and of Maoist revolutionary movements worldwide. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a> of the 1960s, the Chinese and Soviet parties each claimed to be the sole heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>. The Albanian Party of Labour sided early with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and Maoist groups arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Bangladesh and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, where the Communist Party of the Philippines was founded on 26 December 1968. In Peru the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> fought an insurgency in the 1980s and 1990s that resulted in tens of thousands of deaths.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> described the true spirit of Mao Zedong Thought as seeking truth from facts, and made clear that Mao was fallible, judging him 70 percent right and 30 percent wrong.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maoism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
